/-!
# Formal theorem index

This module is the machine-checked entry point corresponding to section 14 of
the prose semantics.  Only established theorems are re-exported here.  The
complete, staged obligation inventory lives in `THEOREMS.md`; entries move
from that inventory into this module as their semantic layer is encoded.

Established in the initial vertical slice:

* direct method selection is functional;
* a superclass-chain witness is unique;
* unrestricted, public, and protected lookup are deterministic;
* the U/Pub/Prot named rules equal their executable definitions; and
* elaborated class binding equals the ECOOP-style class scan under the stated
  static scope/parent correspondence;
* nearest application and lexical depth are deterministic; and
* ordinary dispatch, super dispatch, and DNU target selection are
  deterministic for well-formed states;
* enclosing-receiver, outer, and self dispatch are deterministic; and
* elaborated and ECOOP-style class-scope implicit dispatch are dynamically
  equivalent;
* complete annotated implicit dispatch and all request kinds are
  deterministic; and
* DNU message-mirror allocation is fresh, preserves its supply invariant, and
  yields deterministic complete-request transitions; and
* method activation allocation preserves both supplies and tail/non-tail
  invocation is deterministic; and
* receiver/argument order, request completion, and the current sequential-rule
  union are deterministic; and
* local initialization (including simultaneous groups), sequencing, direct
  local operations, and ordinary completion are deterministic; and
* local/non-local return marks exactly the continuation-dependent set and is
  deterministic, including `cannotReturn`.
* closure creation captures its lexical/runtime context, direct closure-value
  dispatch is disjoint from ordinary lookup, and tail/non-tail closure
  invocation is deterministic.
* the completed sequential fragment preserves global heap coherence, all
  allocation frontiers, stack-activation liveness, and invocation-class
  admissibility.
* every runtime heap identity store has an exact finite domain, and reflective
  enumeration of a class's instances and a mixin's applications is executable,
  exact, and duplicate-free.
* unconditional strong references are structurally enumerable for every base
  heap record, and each finite reachability iteration has an exact relational
  characterization.
* ordinary objects may carry WeakArray or WeakMap storage; their primitive
  operations are executable, valid reads denote live objects, and changing
  weak storage cannot change unconditional strong references.
* conditional WeakMap reachability has an executable finite iteration proved
  equivalent to, and least among, sets closed under the ephemeron rule.
* atomic collection retains exactly the conditional closure, clears dead
  WeakArray cells to `nil`, removes dead WeakMap entries, and leaves only
  resolving weak observations.
* collection lifted to the sequential machine preserves allocation history
  and well-formedness under explicit root adequacy, and erases to either a base
  step or a strong-observation stutter.
-/

